@lesson neural-nets
Q: Why does a network need nonlinear activation functions?
* Without them, stacked layers collapse into a single linear map
- They make training faster
- They reduce the number of parameters
- They prevent overfitting
> \(\mathbf W_2(\mathbf W_1\mathbf x)=(\mathbf W_2\mathbf W_1)\mathbf x\): depth adds nothing without nonlinearity.

Q: What is the maximum value of the sigmoid's derivative?
= 0.25 ±0.001
> \(\sigma'(z)=\sigma(1-\sigma)\) peaks at 0.25 when \(z=0\), one reason sigmoids cause vanishing gradients in deep stacks.

Q: Which activation is the standard choice inside Transformer feed-forward layers like BERT and GPT?
- Sigmoid
- Step function
* GELU (or SwiGLU variants)
- Softmax
> GELU is a smooth ReLU-like function; many recent LLMs use SwiGLU.

Q: The universal approximation theorem guarantees that gradient descent will find a good network.
= false
> It only says a good enough network exists, not that training finds it or that it generalizes.

@lesson backprop
Q: In \(\mathbf z=\mathbf W\mathbf h+\mathbf b\) with \(\boldsymbol\delta=\partial\mathcal L/\partial\mathbf z\), what is \(\partial\mathcal L/\partial\mathbf h\)?
- \(\boldsymbol\delta\mathbf h^\top\)
* \(\mathbf W^\top\boldsymbol\delta\)
- \(\mathbf W\boldsymbol\delta\)
- \(\boldsymbol\delta\)
> The error signal flows back through the transposed weights.

Q: How is the error signal passed back through a ReLU?
* Multiplied element-wise by \(\mathbb 1[\mathbf z\gt0]\)
- Multiplied by \(\mathbf z\)
- Unchanged
- Set to zero everywhere
> ReLU's derivative is 1 where the input was positive and 0 elsewhere.

Q: On the graph \(f=(x\cdot y)+z\) with \(x=2, y=-3, z=4\), what is \(\partial f/\partial x\)?
= -3
> \(\partial f/\partial x=y=-3\).

Q: Why is reverse-mode differentiation used for training rather than forward mode?
- It is more accurate
* One backward pass gives the derivative of a scalar loss with respect to all parameters
- Forward mode cannot handle ReLU
- It uses less code
> Forward mode would need one pass per parameter; with millions of parameters that is hopeless.

@lesson training-deep
Q: What weight variance does He initialization use for ReLU layers?
- \(1/n_{in}\)
* \(2/n_{in}\)
- \(2/(n_{in}+n_{out})\)
- 1
> The factor 2 compensates for ReLU zeroing about half of its inputs.

Q: For 10 balanced classes, roughly what should the initial cross-entropy loss be? (natural log)
= 2.303 ±0.05
> A model that predicts uniformly gives \(\ln 10\approx2.30\). A very different value hints at a bug.

Q: Which normalization layer normalizes across features within each example and is standard in Transformers?
- BatchNorm
* LayerNorm (or RMSNorm)
- Dropout
- Weight decay
> It does not depend on batch statistics, so it works with any batch size and sequence length.

Q: Which are good first steps when a new model won't train? (select all)
* Overfit a single small batch
* Check the initial loss value
* Sweep the learning rate on a log scale
- Add more regularization
> Regularization comes after the model can fit at all.

@lesson pytorch-loop
Q: Which line must come between `loss.backward()` and the next forward pass to apply the update?
- `model.eval()`
* `optimizer.step()`
- `loss.item()`
- `torch.no_grad()`
> `step()` updates the parameters using the computed gradients.

Q: What does `torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(params, 1.0)` do?
* Rescales the gradient if its global norm exceeds 1.0
- Sets all gradients to 1
- Removes gradients smaller than 1
- Clips weights to [-1, 1]
> Clipping prevents a single huge gradient from destabilizing training.

Q: You should apply softmax to the model output before passing it to `F.cross_entropy`.
= false
> `F.cross_entropy` takes raw logits and applies a stable log-softmax itself.

@lesson cnn
Q: Input 224×224×3, a convolution with 64 filters of 7×7, stride 2 and padding 3. What is the output height?
= 112
> \(\lfloor(224-7+6)/2\rfloor+1=112\).

Q: How many parameters does that convolution have (including biases)?
= 9472
> \(7\cdot7\cdot3\cdot64+64=9{,}472\).

Q: What is the receptive field of three stacked 3×3 convolutions with stride 1?
- 3×3
- 5×5
* 7×7
- 9×9
> Each layer adds 2 pixels, so 3 → 5 → 7.

Q: Why do residual connections help very deep networks train?
* The identity path gives gradients a direct route backward
- They reduce the number of parameters
- They replace batch normalization
- They make convolutions faster
> \(\partial\mathbf y/\partial\mathbf x=\partial\mathcal F/\partial\mathbf x+\mathbf I\), so the gradient never has to pass only through many small factors.

@lesson rnn
Q: Why do vanilla RNNs struggle with long-range dependencies?
- They have too many parameters
* Gradients through many time steps vanish or explode, like repeated matrix powers
- They can only read one word
- They lack an output layer
> The product of Jacobians behaves like \(\mathbf W_{hh}^{T-t}\).

Q: Which mechanism lets an LSTM preserve information over many steps?
* The additively updated cell state controlled by a forget gate
- The output softmax
- Dropout
- Weight tying
> When the forget gate is near 1, both information and gradients flow through the cell state almost unchanged.

Q: Gradient clipping fixes exploding gradients but not vanishing ones.
= true
> Clipping caps large gradients; vanishing needs architectural fixes such as gating, residuals or attention.

@lesson transformer
Q: Why are attention scores divided by \(\sqrt{d_k}\)?
* To keep the variance of the dot products near 1 so the softmax does not saturate
- To make the scores positive
- To reduce memory use
- To normalize the values
> A dot product of \(d_k\) unit-variance terms has variance \(d_k\).

Q: What does the causal mask do in a decoder?
- Hides padding tokens only
* Prevents each token from attending to later tokens
- Randomly drops attention weights
- Masks the first token
> Setting future scores to \(-\infty\) makes the model a valid next-token predictor.

Q: How does standard self-attention's time and memory scale with sequence length \(n\)?
- \(O(n)\)
- \(O(n\log n)\)
* \(O(n^2)\)
- \(O(n^3)\)
> The score matrix \(\mathbf Q\mathbf K^\top\) is \(n\times n\).

Q: Roughly how many parameters does one Transformer block with model width \(d\) have (ignoring biases and norms)?
- \(4d^2\)
- \(8d^2\)
* \(12d^2\)
- \(d^3\)
> About \(4d^2\) for the attention projections plus \(8d^2\) for a 4× wide feed-forward layer.

Q: Without positional information, self-attention treats its input as a set.
= true
> Permuting the tokens permutes the outputs identically, so order must be injected with positional encodings such as RoPE.

@lesson gnn
Q: Why must a GNN's neighbour aggregation be permutation-invariant (sum, mean, max)?
* Graph neighbours have no inherent order
- To save memory
- To make it differentiable
- Because GPUs require it
> The result must not depend on how nodes happen to be numbered.

Q: What is over-smoothing in deep GNNs?
- Weights become too small
* Node representations become nearly identical after many rounds of averaging
- The graph loses edges
- Training becomes too fast
> Repeated neighbourhood averaging blurs everything together, so most GNNs use only a few layers.

Q: A Transformer can be viewed as a GNN on a fully connected graph of tokens.
= true
> Attention is message passing with learned weights over every pair of tokens.

@lesson ssm
Q: What is the main advantage of state space models and linear attention over standard attention for long sequences?
* Constant memory per generated token and linear scaling in sequence length
- Higher accuracy on every task
- No parameters
- They need no training
> A fixed-size recurrent state replaces the growing KV cache.

Q: A linear time-invariant SSM can be trained in parallel because it can be written as…
- a decision tree
* a convolution over the input sequence
- a softmax
- a lookup table
> Unrolling the recurrence gives a convolution kernel \((\mathbf C\bar{\mathbf B},\mathbf C\bar{\mathbf A}\bar{\mathbf B},\dots)\).

Q: Why do many long-context models mix a few attention layers into an SSM stack?
* Pure SSMs can struggle with exact recall of specific earlier tokens
- Attention layers are cheaper
- SSMs cannot be trained
- To reduce parameters
> Attention retrieves precise tokens easily; hybrids combine that with SSM efficiency.

@lesson ssl
Q: In contrastive learning, what prevents representation collapse?
- A larger learning rate
* Negative examples (or stop-gradient and momentum-teacher tricks)
- Removing augmentations
- Using a smaller batch
> Without negatives or asymmetry, mapping everything to one vector trivially makes views agree.

Q: What do data augmentations define in contrastive learning?
* Which differences the representation learns to ignore
- The number of classes
- The learning rate
- The test set
> Crops, colour jitter and blur teach invariance to exactly those changes.

Q: What is a linear probe?
- Fine-tuning every layer
* Training only a linear classifier on top of frozen features
- A probe that measures GPU temperature
- A kind of data augmentation
> It measures how linearly accessible useful information is in the representation.

@lesson compression
Q: Why does knowledge distillation multiply the KL term by \(T^2\)?
* Softening by temperature \(T\) scales gradients down by \(1/T^2\); the factor restores their size
- To make the loss positive
- To increase the temperature
- It has no effect
> This keeps the soft and hard losses balanced across temperatures.

Q: Which pruning gives real speedups on ordinary GPUs without special sparse kernels?
- Unstructured weight pruning
* Structured pruning of whole channels, heads or layers
- Random pruning
- Pruning biases only
> Removing whole structures shrinks dense matrix sizes, which standard hardware benefits from.

Q: Quantization-aware training usually recovers more accuracy than post-training quantization at very low bit-widths.
= true
> The model learns to tolerate rounding during training.

@lesson exam
Q: A 50-layer plain CNN trains worse than a 20-layer one even on the training set. What is the most direct fix?
- More dropout
* Residual connections
- A smaller learning rate only
- Removing batch normalization
> This degradation problem motivated ResNets: identity shortcuts make deeper networks at least as trainable.

Q: Count the parameters of a single-head attention layer with \(d=64\) (Q, K, V and output projections, no biases).
= 16384
> Four \(64\times64\) matrices: \(4\cdot4096=16{,}384\).

Q: Your loss becomes NaN after a few hundred steps of fp16 training. Which fixes are reasonable? (select all)
* Switch to bf16 or enable dynamic loss scaling
* Lower the learning rate and add gradient clipping
* Use fused, numerically stable loss functions on logits
- Remove all normalization layers
> Instability comes from overflow, underflow and too-large updates.

Q: Which architecture best fits a molecule-toxicity prediction task?
- A CNN on a fixed grid
* A graph neural network with graph-level pooling
- A plain RNN over atom names
- k-means
> Molecules are graphs of atoms and bonds; GNNs respect that structure.

Q: In a Transformer block, the feed-forward layer mixes information across tokens.
= false
> The FFN processes each token independently; attention is what mixes across tokens.
